1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,310
On November the twentieth,
twenty twenty-six, Levent Alpöge,

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:10,250
a mathematician at the AI company
Anthropic, posted a four-page note.

3
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:15,080
In it, he writes that he believes he has
found a proof of the Riemann hypothesis,

4
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:17,740
working with the company's AI model,
Claude.

5
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:19,760
Instead of a paper,

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,190
the note links to about a hundred
and seventy-five thousand files in Lean,

7
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,650
a language in which a computer
checks every step of a proof.

8
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:31,100
As I record this,
those checks are still under way.

9
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:35,750
The Riemann hypothesis has been
open since eighteen fifty-nine.

10
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:39,490
It carries a million-dollar prize,
and many mathematicians consider

11
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:43,030
it the most important open
problem in pure mathematics.

12
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:47,280
To judge the claim,
we need to know what the hypothesis says.

13
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:49,590
That starts with the prime numbers.

14
00:00:50,190 --> 00:00:54,170
These are the primes below a hundred,
the numbers above one that can only

15
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:57,130
be divided evenly by one and themselves.

16
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:59,420
Count them as you go, and the count climbs

17
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:04,740
like a staircase, one step per prime,
at places no simple rule predicts.

18
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:09,900
Here is a smooth curve that follows
the overall trend, but has no steps.

19
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:15,290
Now I'll add a single wave to it.
Then five, then thirty, then a hundred.

20
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:16,667
With each batch,

21
00:01:16,693 --> 00:01:21,630
the curve hugs the staircase more closely,
until it turns a corner at every prime.

22
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:25,280
Each wave comes from one point,
drawn on the right.

23
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:29,970
These points are zeros of something called
the Riemann zeta function, and every one

24
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,620
we used sits on the same vertical line.

25
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:37,180
The Riemann hypothesis says that all
of them do, all infinitely many.

26
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:41,600
That takes some unpacking:
the zeta function, its zeros,

27
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:45,690
why the hypothesis is so hard to prove,
and what a proof would mean.

28
00:01:45,830 --> 00:01:49,080
Then we'll come back to November,
so you can judge for yourself what

29
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:51,280
has been established, and what hasn't.

30
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:58,150
Every whole number above one can be built
by multiplying primes, in only one way.

31
00:01:58,290 --> 00:02:01,250
Sixty is two times two times three times

32
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:05,130
five, and apart from the order,
there's no other way to build it.

33
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,730
The primes are the atoms of arithmetic.

34
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:12,580
More than two thousand years ago, Euclid
showed that the primes never run out.

35
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:15,290
Multiply any list of them and add one.

36
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:18,800
Dividing by any prime on the list
leaves a remainder of one,

37
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,530
so the result's prime factors are all new.

38
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:24,000
Finding them is mechanical.

39
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,680
Write out the numbers up to a hundred,
keep two,

40
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:28,710
and cross out its other multiples.

41
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:33,440
Do the same for three, five and seven,
and what survives are the primes.

42
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:37,800
This is the sieve of Eratosthenes,
and it will come back in a different form.

43
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:40,290
Predicting them is another matter.

44
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,880
Some come in close pairs,
like eleven and thirteen,

45
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:48,920
while after eighty-nine,
seven numbers go by with no prime at all.

46
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,350
Over long stretches,
though, there's a pattern.

47
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,410
Around seventeen ninety-two,
a teenage Carl Friedrich Gauss

48
00:02:55,460 --> 00:03:00,530
noticed in tables of primes that a number
near x is prime with a chance of about one

49
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,490
over the natural log of x.

50
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:06,040
Near a million,
that's about one in fourteen.

51
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:10,720
Adding up those chances gives a smooth
curve, the logarithmic integral.

52
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:14,860
At a million, it's within two tenths
of a percent of the true count.

53
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:19,510
In eighteen ninety-six, Jacques Hadamard
and Charles de la Vallée Poussin each

54
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:24,010
proved that the ratio of the two goes
to one: the prime number theorem.

55
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:28,150
Both proofs used the zeta function,
and both needed one fact about where

56
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:30,520
its zeros can't be.

57
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:34,010
Still, the staircase never
matches the curve exactly.

58
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:35,990
How far apart can they get?

59
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:39,070
That question is what
the Riemann hypothesis is about.

60
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:42,190
Here's the function.

61
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:46,320
Pick a number s, and add up one,
plus one over two to the s,

62
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:49,857
plus one over three to the s,
and so on forever.

63
00:03:49,907 --> 00:03:51,910
The total is zeta of s.

64
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:56,600
A sum like this only has a value
if its running total settles down.

65
00:03:56,830 --> 00:04:00,490
When s is one, it doesn't:
the total grows without limit.

66
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:04,770
When s is two, it settles,
and in seventeen thirty-four Leonhard

67
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:08,140
Euler found the value:
pi squared over six.

68
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:11,380
Euler also found the link to the primes.

69
00:04:11,470 --> 00:04:15,550
Multiply the sum by one minus one
over two to the s, and every term

70
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:17,550
with an even number cancels.

71
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,680
Multiply by one minus one over three

72
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:22,890
to the s, and the remaining
multiples of three cancel.

73
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:24,537
It's the sieve again.

74
00:04:24,587 --> 00:04:27,670
Do this for every prime,
and only the one is left.

75
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:32,990
So a sum over all whole numbers equals
a product over the primes, and zeta

76
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:35,400
carries information about every prime.

77
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:39,647
In eighteen fifty-nine,
Bernhard Riemann took the next step,

78
00:04:39,673 --> 00:04:43,610
in a nine-page paper, the only one
he ever published on number theory.

79
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:47,650
He let s be a complex number:
a point in a plane, where

80
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:52,300
the real part says how far right it sits,
and the imaginary part says how high.

81
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:55,290
Then each term becomes an arrow.

82
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:59,480
The real part of s sets its length,
and the imaginary part turns it,

83
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:02,200
by an angle that grows
with the logarithm of n.

84
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:05,987
Laid end to end,
the arrows curl into a spiral,

85
00:05:06,013 --> 00:05:10,620
and when the real part is more than one,
the spiral closes in on a single point:

86
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:12,200
zeta of s.

87
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:16,890
The outputs are complex too,
so a graph would need four dimensions.

88
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:21,530
Instead, we color the plane:
each point gets a hue for the direction

89
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:24,970
of zeta's value there,
and bands that mark its size.

90
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:28,220
Where all the colors meet,
the value is zero.

91
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:30,210
That's all a zero is:

92
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:33,400
an input where the output is exactly zero.

93
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,590
This is zeta where the sum works.

94
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:39,177
Left of the line where
the real part is one,

95
00:05:39,227 --> 00:05:43,040
the arrows don't shrink fast enough,
and the spiral never closes.

96
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:46,140
A simpler sum has the same problem.

97
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:51,250
One plus x plus x squared, and so on,
only adds up when x is between minus

98
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:52,780
one and one.

99
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:55,597
There it equals one over one minus x,

100
00:05:55,647 --> 00:05:58,550
and that expression works
for every x except one.

101
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,860
The formula keeps going
where the sum stops.

102
00:06:02,450 --> 00:06:06,490
Functions like zeta,
smooth in the complex sense, are rigid:

103
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:11,847
know one on any small patch of the plane,
and there's at most one way to extend it.

104
00:06:11,853 --> 00:06:16,067
Riemann found the extension of zeta
to the whole plane, except the point one,

105
00:06:16,093 --> 00:06:18,280
and a symmetry linking its value at s

106
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:22,310
to its value at one minus s,
called the functional equation.

107
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,950
So where is zeta zero?

108
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,980
The functional equation contains a sine,
and that sine makes zeta zero at minus

109
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:35,330
two, minus four, minus six,
and every negative even number.

110
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:38,770
These are the trivial zeros,
and they're well understood.

111
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:44,510
Every other zero lies in a vertical band
between real part zero and real part one,

112
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,110
called the critical strip.

113
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:51,050
Its center line, where the real part
is exactly one half, is the critical line.

114
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:55,740
Moving up the line, the first zero
is at a height of about fourteen,

115
00:06:55,790 --> 00:07:00,680
then twenty-one, twenty-five, thirty,
thirty-three and thirty-eight.

116
00:07:00,790 --> 00:07:03,130
Each one is exactly on the line.

117
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:05,690
Zeta's symmetries mirror the zeros across

118
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:08,880
this line, so a zero off it
would come with a partner.

119
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:11,440
No such pair has ever been found.

120
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:16,110
Riemann's private notes show he
computed the first few zeros by hand.

121
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:20,520
In his paper, he wrote that it's very
likely all of them lie on this line,

122
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:24,510
and that after a few quick attempts,
he had put aside the search for a proof.

123
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:29,980
That's the Riemann hypothesis: every
nontrivial zero has real part one half.

124
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:34,120
David Hilbert put it on his famous
list of problems in nineteen hundred,

125
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,440
and in two thousand it became
a Millennium Prize Problem.

126
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:42,880
How can anyone know a zero is exactly
on the line, not just very near it?

127
00:07:43,090 --> 00:07:47,410
Along the line, zeta can be turned
into a real-valued wave, Hardy's Z

128
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:52,460
function, and every time it crosses zero,
there's a zero exactly on the line.

129
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:54,530
If the crossings match a separate count

130
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:58,630
of all the zeros below some height,
every one of them is on the line.

131
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:03,187
Alan Turing ran some of the first checks
like this on an electronic computer,

132
00:08:03,213 --> 00:08:04,850
in the early nineteen fifties.

133
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:06,360
Since then it's been done

134
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:10,680
for the first ten trillion zeros,
and every one is on the line.

135
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:14,770
Now the waves from the opening make sense.

136
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:18,850
In the same paper, Riemann wrote down
an exact formula for the prime-counting

137
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:23,060
staircase: a smooth part,
plus one wave for each zero.

138
00:08:23,610 --> 00:08:26,480
A zero's height sets how
fast its wave wiggles.

139
00:08:26,530 --> 00:08:29,600
Its real part sets how
fast the wave grows.

140
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:31,930
For a zero with real part one half,

141
00:08:31,980 --> 00:08:35,220
the wave's size grows roughly
like the square root of x.

142
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:40,220
The fact the prime number theorem needed
was that no zero has real part one.

143
00:08:40,270 --> 00:08:42,490
That's enough to make all
the waves together grow

144
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:44,590
more slowly than the count itself.

145
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:47,050
The hypothesis asks for much more.

146
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:51,640
Suppose one zero sat off the line,
at real part point eight.

147
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:55,947
Its wave would grow like x to the point
eight, faster than all the others,

148
00:08:55,973 --> 00:08:59,820
and over long ranges it would pull
the staircase far away from the curve.

149
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:03,340
If every zero is on the line,
that never happens,

150
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:07,460
and the gap stays below roughly
the square root of x times its logarithm.

151
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:08,717
In nineteen oh one,

152
00:09:08,767 --> 00:09:13,087
Helge von Koch showed the reverse too:
if the gap always stays that small,

153
00:09:13,113 --> 00:09:14,800
the hypothesis is true.

154
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:16,940
And since zeros come in mirrored pairs,

155
00:09:16,990 --> 00:09:20,300
one half is the smallest
the largest real part could be.

156
00:09:20,350 --> 00:09:22,490
So the hypothesis says the primes stay

157
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:25,080
as close to the curve
as they possibly can.

158
00:09:26,610 --> 00:09:30,390
If ten trillion zeros are on the line,
why isn't that a proof?

159
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:33,990
Because there are infinitely many,
and in number theory a pattern can

160
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:36,560
hold for a very long time and then fail.

161
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:38,030
The prime count stays below

162
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:42,030
the logarithmic integral for every number
anyone has checked, but in nineteen

163
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:46,310
fourteen John Littlewood proved
that the order flips infinitely often.

164
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:49,450
The first flip lies somewhere
past ten quintillion,

165
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:53,440
possibly as far out as a number
with over three hundred digits.

166
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:57,290
The zeros also sit where
the original sum stops working.

167
00:09:57,340 --> 00:09:59,730
Where the sum works,
the product over primes

168
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:02,420
shows at once that zeta can't be zero.

169
00:10:02,470 --> 00:10:05,790
Inside the strip,
that product no longer converges.

170
00:10:06,150 --> 00:10:08,830
And the zeros come close
to breaking the rule.

171
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:12,990
In nineteen fifty-six,
Derrick Lehmer found two zeros near height

172
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:17,600
seven thousand and five, about twenty-four
times closer together than usual.

173
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:20,950
Between them,
the Z wave climbs only four thousandths

174
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:22,790
above zero before turning back.

175
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:26,950
If it had turned back just short of zero,
the two zeros would have had to leave

176
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,640
the line as a mirror pair.

177
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:30,203
In twenty eighteen,

178
00:10:30,253 --> 00:10:33,830
Brad Rodgers and Terence Tao proved
a precise version of this idea:

179
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:38,100
if the hypothesis is true,
it's true with no room to spare.

180
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:40,940
Progress has also been slow.

181
00:10:41,100 --> 00:10:44,540
One measure is the fraction
of zeros proven to lie on the line.

182
00:10:44,590 --> 00:10:49,210
Hardy showed in nineteen fourteen
that infinitely many do, and from nineteen

183
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:52,250
forty-two to twenty twenty,
the proven fraction crept

184
00:10:52,300 --> 00:10:56,210
from a sliver to five twelfths,
just under forty-two percent.

185
00:10:56,440 --> 00:11:01,050
Even a hundred percent wouldn't be enough:
in an infinite set, a hundred percent can

186
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,050
still leave out infinitely many.

187
00:11:03,790 --> 00:11:08,777
Finally, a version of the hypothesis has
been proved, and it shows what's missing.

188
00:11:08,827 --> 00:11:12,680
Take an equation like this one and count
its solutions in clock arithmetic,

189
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:15,220
where numbers wrap around at a prime p.

190
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:19,480
The count never strays from p by more
than twice the square root of p,

191
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:23,100
the same square-root behavior
the hypothesis predicts for the primes.

192
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:27,320
André Weil proved bounds like this for all
curves in the nineteen forties,

193
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:31,250
and Pierre Deligne a far more general
version in nineteen seventy-four.

194
00:11:31,430 --> 00:11:35,590
Their proofs use geometry with no known
counterpart for the whole numbers.

195
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:39,230
Solving it means one of two things:

196
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:43,850
a proof that covers every zero at once,
including the ones no computer will ever

197
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:48,060
reach, or a single zero off the line,
which would disprove it.

198
00:11:48,790 --> 00:11:52,510
A proof would make the gap bound
a theorem, and turn a great many results

199
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:57,070
that begin with "assume the Riemann
hypothesis" into unconditional ones, about

200
00:11:57,120 --> 00:11:59,520
the gaps between primes and much else.

201
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:03,800
Some results need a stronger version,
the generalized Riemann hypothesis,

202
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:07,170
which makes the same claim
for a family of related functions.

203
00:12:07,310 --> 00:12:09,680
A proof wouldn't break encryption, though.

204
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:11,470
Knowing how the primes
are spread out doesn't

205
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:13,720
tell you how to factor a large number.

206
00:12:14,410 --> 00:12:15,960
Some ideas go further.

207
00:12:16,010 --> 00:12:18,850
In the nineteen seventies,
Hugh Montgomery and Freeman

208
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:21,090
Dyson noticed that the gaps between

209
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:26,130
zeros seem to follow the same statistics
as energy levels in heavy atomic nuclei.

210
00:12:26,250 --> 00:12:29,260
Some see a hint of a hidden
physical system.

211
00:12:29,300 --> 00:12:32,080
That's speculation,
and a proof wouldn't settle it.

212
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:34,770
Back to this year.

213
00:12:34,820 --> 00:12:37,790
In August,
Anthropic announced that an unreleased

214
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:41,790
research version of Claude had found
a proof raising the proven fraction

215
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:44,620
from five twelfths to two thirds.

216
00:12:44,670 --> 00:12:49,260
Alpöge and his Anthropic colleague
Ralph Furman checked it and wrote it up.

217
00:12:49,310 --> 00:12:53,320
Brian Conrey and Dan Goldston,
experts on exactly this problem,

218
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,780
examined it, and it was checked in Lean.

219
00:12:56,100 --> 00:13:00,690
A few weeks later, Youness Lamzouri
posted a simpler, independent proof.

220
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:02,430
Anthropic said it didn't expect

221
00:13:02,480 --> 00:13:05,110
these methods to lead
to the full hypothesis.

222
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:09,580
Then came November.
Here's what Lean actually checks.

223
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:12,530
Definitions, statements and proofs are all

224
00:13:12,580 --> 00:13:17,010
written as code, and a small program
called the kernel checks that each step

225
00:13:17,060 --> 00:13:20,260
follows from earlier ones,
back to a few basic axioms.

226
00:13:20,310 --> 00:13:24,990
If the kernel accepts a proof,
a broken step is very unlikely.

227
00:13:25,610 --> 00:13:28,470
What the kernel can't tell you
is whether the final statement

228
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:30,400
means what you think it means.

229
00:13:30,450 --> 00:13:32,710
Kevin Buzzard, a mathematician at Imperial

230
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:36,970
College London, says it's the first
question to ask of any Lean proof.

231
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:40,390
Here is the main statement,
and four more theorems extend it

232
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:43,330
to a family called Dirichlet L-functions.

233
00:13:43,380 --> 00:13:47,670
The main one uses the definition
of zeta from Mathlib, a large shared

234
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:52,240
library of Lean mathematics, so part
of the job is confirming the definitions.

235
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:55,110
One test avoids that definition.

236
00:13:55,310 --> 00:13:59,020
In nineteen eighty-four, Guy Robin
showed the hypothesis is equivalent

237
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:01,770
to an inequality about sums of divisors,

238
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:06,570
for every number past five thousand
and forty, with no zeta in it at all.

239
00:14:06,620 --> 00:14:10,100
The proof has been joined to a separate
formalization of Robin's result,

240
00:14:10,140 --> 00:14:12,510
and that combination is being checked too.

241
00:14:12,970 --> 00:14:17,790
So far, Andrew Sutherland at MIT has
rebuilt all hundred and seventy-five

242
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:21,650
thousand files and run them through
several independent checkers.

243
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:24,510
Every check that has finished
has accepted the proof, and he

244
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:29,640
told Scientific American that the chance
anything is fishy is approximately zero.

245
00:14:29,690 --> 00:14:32,920
A few of the slowest were still
running when I recorded this.

246
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:35,690
There's no human-readable paper yet.

247
00:14:35,890 --> 00:14:39,490
Alpöge writes that the argument is very
long at the moment, and that he's

248
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:43,330
presenting it before having mastered
much of the mathematics around it.

249
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:45,597
By his account, he steered the search,

250
00:14:45,647 --> 00:14:48,420
and Claude far outpaced
him near the finish line.

251
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:51,190
He calls the argument
a descendant of a method

252
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:56,030
Sergei Stepanov found in the late nineteen
sixties for the clock-arithmetic version.

253
00:14:56,150 --> 00:14:58,230
That method needs functions that vanish

254
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:02,497
many times over at each solution,
which takes something like derivatives,

255
00:15:02,547 --> 00:15:05,300
and whole numbers have
no obvious derivative.

256
00:15:05,430 --> 00:15:08,560
The new argument,
he writes, finds a stand-in.

257
00:15:09,350 --> 00:15:11,910
Officially, the problem is still open.

258
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:16,197
The Clay Institute considers a solution
only after it has been published,

259
00:15:16,247 --> 00:15:19,620
has stood for two years,
and has been broadly accepted.

260
00:15:19,670 --> 00:15:23,710
Until then,
this is a serious claim under examination.

261
00:15:24,390 --> 00:15:27,180
So we have several
different kinds of results.

262
00:15:27,390 --> 00:15:30,300
Ten trillion zeros
on the line is evidence.

263
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:34,220
Two thirds is a theorem,
checked by people and by machine.

264
00:15:34,270 --> 00:15:39,210
The full hypothesis has a proposed proof,
with machine checks partly complete.

265
00:15:39,260 --> 00:15:43,890
The last stage, people understanding
why it works, has barely begun.

266
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:47,300
Let's go back to the staircase.

267
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:50,820
Riemann found that the primes,
irregular as they look,

268
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:55,520
are built exactly from waves,
one for each zero of a single function.

269
00:15:55,570 --> 00:16:00,567
If every zero is on the critical line,
every wave grows as slowly as it can,

270
00:16:00,593 --> 00:16:03,560
and the primes never stray
far from the smooth curve.

271
00:16:04,310 --> 00:16:08,030
In eighteen fifty-nine,
Riemann put the search for a proof aside.

272
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:13,080
If this claim holds, that search ended
a hundred and sixty-seven years later,

273
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:17,040
with a proof that mathematicians
are only beginning to read.
